# FinModel — a kit the whole team imports

The [[Scoring a lead]] unit answers one question — *how good is this lead?* — with
a single number. But the moment sales wants to reason about money, one number is
not enough: what is a customer worth over their lifetime, what did it cost to win
them, is the deal worth doing once you discount future cash back to today? Those
three calculations show up in the [[Dashboard]], in the agents' [[The Analyst Agent]]
drafts, and inside `:score` itself. We do **not** want three copies of the same
LTV formula drifting apart across the workbook.

So this file does the thing the folder name promises: it authors a **kit**. A kit
is the format's word for *a reusable library you import* — and on the locked model
a kit is not a manifest, not a config, not a special construct. **A kit is a plain
Elixir module that exports a prefix of related helpers.** When that module declares
an `@behaviour`, the behaviour *is* the shared interface — the contract other units
can depend on. There is no WIT to write and no schema to maintain: the module's
public functions are the exports, its `@callback`s are the interface, and the weave
compiles the whole thing to a standard wasm component so any other unit, in any
language, can call it. The rule holds here as everywhere — **learn Elixir, not WIT.**

## The shared interface — an `@behaviour`

First we name the contract. A behaviour lists the functions any financial model
*must* provide; it is the kit's published surface and the thing the [[Code graph]]
records as a `type` edge when another unit imports us. We deal in two kinds of
input: a `%Lead{}` (the canonical struct from [[Lead]]) for top-of-funnel value,
and a plain `Deal` record for a specific opportunity with its own economics.

server :finmodel do
  defmodule Deal do
    # A concrete opportunity. `cash_flows` are the projected yearly net inflows
    # *after* the deal closes; `acquisition_cost` is what we spent to win it.
    defstruct lead: nil, acquisition_cost: 0, cash_flows: [], discount_rate: 0.12
  end

  defmodule FinModel do
    @moduledoc """
    The shared interface every financial model implements. Importing `FinModel`
    gives a consumer the `ltv/1`, `cac/1`, and `npv/2` prefix; the behaviour
    below is the contract those consumers may rely on.
    """

    @doc "Lifetime value of a lead, in whole currency units."
    @callback ltv(Lead.t()) :: number()

    @doc "Customer acquisition cost for a closed deal."
    @callback cac(Deal.t()) :: number()

    @doc "Net present value of a deal's cash flows at a discount rate."
    @callback npv(Deal.t(), float()) :: number()
  end
end

Two things worth pausing on. The `@callback` lines are the *only* place in this
file we lean on an annotation, and even here they are documentation of the
interface, not a typespec DSL — the real types are the `%Lead{}` and `%Deal{}`
structs themselves, inferred from their `defstruct` defaults. And `Deal` is a
**type** in exactly the sense the format means it: a `defstruct`, field types
inferred from defaults (`acquisition_cost` is an integer because `0` is, `cash_flows`
is a list because `[]` is). No `type X = record(...)`, no `@type` leading the file.
Structure, not a schema language.

## The implementation — the prefix of helpers

Now the model itself. `Default` implements the `FinModel` behaviour, which means
the compiler checks we actually provide `ltv/1`, `cac/1`, and `npv/2` with the
right shapes — a missing callback is caught at the weave gate, not in production.

server :finmodel do
  defmodule Default do
    @behaviour FinModel

    # Tunables for the LTV heuristic. A lead's lifetime value scales with its
    # revenue (bigger accounts pay more) and the team-size signal (more seats),
    # against a blended gross margin and an expected retention horizon in years.
    @gross_margin 0.80
    @retention_years 3.0
    @seat_value 1_200

    @impl FinModel
    def ltv(%Lead{revenue: revenue, employees: employees}) do
      # Annual contribution = a slice of account revenue + a per-seat expansion
      # signal, kept at gross margin, then projected over the retention horizon.
      annual_revenue = revenue * 0.10 + employees * @seat_value
      annual_margin = annual_revenue * @gross_margin

      (annual_margin * @retention_years)
      |> Float.round(2)
    end

    @impl FinModel
    def cac(%Deal{acquisition_cost: cost}) when cost >= 0 do
      # CAC is what we spent to land the deal. Trivial today, but it lives behind
      # the interface so a future model can amortise channel + headcount costs
      # without a single caller changing.
      cost * 1.0
    end

    @impl FinModel
    def npv(%Deal{cash_flows: flows}, rate) when is_float(rate) do
      # Discount each future yearly inflow back to today: flow / (1 + r)^year,
      # summed. Year 1 is the first projected flow; the present-day acquisition
      # cost is handled separately by `cac/1`, so it does not appear here.
      flows
      |> Enum.with_index(1)
      |> Enum.reduce(0.0, fn {flow, year}, acc ->
        acc + flow / :math.pow(1 + rate, year)
      end)
      |> Float.round(2)
    end

    @impl FinModel
    def npv(%Deal{} = deal, _rate) do
      # Fall back to the deal's own declared discount rate when none is passed.
      npv(deal, deal.discount_rate)
    end
  end
end

The interface returns either an `{:ok, _}` style answer or a raw number depending
on the call; here every helper returns a plain number, and the guards (`when cost
>= 0`, `when is_float(rate)`) are the type story doing real work — a malformed
input simply has no matching clause, which the runtime surfaces as a `{:error,
:no_match}` variant rather than a silent wrong answer.

## Using the kit — import it, call its prefix

A kit earns its keep at the call site. Any other unit *imports* `FinModel.Default`
and gets the `ltv` / `cac` / `npv` prefix as if it were local. The
[[Scoring a lead]] unit can blend lifetime value into its ranking, and the
[[Dashboard]] can show money next to the raw `:score`. Here is `:score`'s sibling
lane reaching for the kit — note there are no new grants: `FinModel` is pure
arithmetic, so importing it adds an `import` edge to the [[Code graph]] but asks
the Dock for nothing.

server :finance_panel do
  import FinModel.Default, only: [ltv: 1, npv: 2]

  alias FinModel.Deal

  @leads [
    %Lead{name: "Acme",    domain: "acme.co",    revenue: 50_000, employees: 20, score: 70, status: :scored},
    %Lead{name: "Globex",  domain: "globex.io",  revenue: 12_000, employees: 8,  score: 41, status: :enriched},
    %Lead{name: "Initech", domain: "initech.com", revenue: 90_000, employees: 55, score: 88, status: :scored}
  ]

  # A money-aware ranking: weight each lead's raw score by its modelled lifetime
  # value, so a high-LTV account beats an equally-scored but smaller one.
  def ranked_by_value do
    @leads
    |> Enum.map(fn lead -> {lead, ltv(lead)} end)
    |> Enum.sort_by(fn {_lead, value} -> value end, :desc)
  end

  # Worked example for the Initech deal: three years of projected upside, half a
  # year's revenue spent to win it, discounted at the deal's own rate.
  def initech_deal do
    initech = Enum.find(@leads, &(&1.name == "Initech"))

    deal = %Deal{
      lead: initech,
      acquisition_cost: 18_000,
      cash_flows: [40_000, 55_000, 60_000],
      discount_rate: 0.12
    }

    %{
      lead: initech.name,
      ltv: ltv(initech),
      npv: npv(deal, deal.discount_rate)
    }
  end
end

## The gate keeps the model honest

Because everything here is pure, it is testable, and the weave gate runs the test
before it emits a bundle — a red financial model ships no `salesapp.html`. We pin
the properties that actually matter: LTV rises with account size, NPV of positive
future cash is positive but *less* than the undiscounted sum (that is the whole
point of discounting), and a zero-rate NPV is just the plain sum.

test :finmodel do
  import FinModel.Default
  alias FinModel.Deal

  @initech %Lead{name: "Initech", domain: "initech.com", revenue: 90_000, employees: 55, score: 88, status: :scored}
  @globex  %Lead{name: "Globex",  domain: "globex.io",  revenue: 12_000, employees: 8,  score: 41, status: :enriched}

  test "LTV grows with the size of the account" do
    assert ltv(@initech) > ltv(@globex)
    assert ltv(%Lead{}) == 0.0
  end

  test "discounting shrinks future cash" do
    deal = %Deal{cash_flows: [10_000, 10_000, 10_000], discount_rate: 0.12}
    undiscounted = Enum.sum(deal.cash_flows)

    assert npv(deal, 0.12) > 0
    assert npv(deal, 0.12) < undiscounted
  end

  test "a zero discount rate is just the sum of flows" do
    deal = %Deal{cash_flows: [5_000, 7_000, 3_000]}
    assert npv(deal, 0.0) == 15_000.0
  end

  test "cac is what we paid" do
    assert cac(%Deal{acquisition_cost: 18_000}) == 18_000.0
  end
end

## Where this fits

`FinModel` is the workbook's money vocabulary. The [[Pipeline]] enriches and
scores leads; this kit turns those leads and the deals they become into LTV, CAC,
and NPV. The [[Dashboard]] imports it to rank by value, the [[The Analyst Agent]] agents
import it to decide which accounts justify a hand-written draft, and `:score` can
fold `ltv/1` in as a signal. Author the formula **once**, behind a `@behaviour`,
and every consumer rides the same numbers — which is exactly what a kit is for.
